WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.993
Welcome to mCoding,

00:00:00.993 --> 00:00:03.801
where we try to get&nbsp;just a little 
 bit better at programming every episode

00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:07.355
so that we can have an excuse for&nbsp;
writing on the window with dry erase markers.

00:00:07.355 --> 00:00:09.886
I'm James Murphy, available 
 for Python&nbsp;consulting.

00:00:09.886 --> 00:00:12.039
Check me out at mcoding.io.

00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:15.010
There's one feature of Python 
 that has&nbsp;not aged well.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.097
So poorly in fact that even Python's 
 creator, Guido van Rossum,

00:00:18.097 --> 00:00:21.760
wishes&nbsp;that it was 
 never added to the language.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.884
That feature is the `else` clause 
 of `for` and&nbsp;`while` loops.

00:00:25.884 --> 00:00:31.152
Unlike `if-else`, `for-else` and `while-else` 
 are very uncommonly used.

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.504
Honestly,&nbsp;comment now if 
 you've never heard of these before.

00:00:34.504 --> 00:00:38.000
So first off, what does 
 this&nbsp;`else` clause actually do?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.480
The `else` clause of a `for` or `while` is&nbsp;
triggered after the loop terminates normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.377
meaning without a break or exception.

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:50.393
So in&nbsp;both of these cases, 
 it'll print 0, 1, 2, "DONE".

00:00:50.393 --> 00:00:56.160
Whereas in either of these cases, it'll&nbsp;
just print 0, 1 and then it will not print "DONE".

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.332
Also, if there are no elements 
 to loop over,

00:00:58.332 --> 00:01:01.886
this is still considered a normal end 
 of the `for` or `while` loop.

00:01:01.886 --> 00:01:05.520
So, the `else` will&nbsp;still be 
 executed in both of these cases.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.800
By now I hope that you're asking&nbsp;
yourself something along the lines of:

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.129
"Okay... why is this called else?"

00:01:13.129 --> 00:01:16.240
I mean,&nbsp;if something else something 
 makes sense.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.076
For something else something or while something&nbsp;
else something...

00:01:20.076 --> 00:01:21.680
What does that really mean?

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.520
And why does it correspond to this behavior 
 of&nbsp;executing if there was no break or exception?

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.859
Way back in the day, Python was 
 much less&nbsp;popular.

00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:34.123
And it was not expected to be the 
 only programming language that you knew.

00:01:34.123 --> 00:01:38.909
It was&nbsp;basically understood that anyone 
 using Python was already familiar with C or C++.

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:44.000
And in particular&nbsp;how compilers
 implemented while loops in C/C++.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.526
In C and C++, a while-loop could &nbsp;
just be implemented in terms of&nbsp;an if check

00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:53.280
with the same corresponding&nbsp;
condition and a go-to statement.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.242
You label just before the start of the loop,&nbsp;
evaluate the condition.

00:01:57.242 --> 00:02:01.043
Run the body of the loop and then 
 go back to the start to repeat the loop.

00:02:01.043 --> 00:02:06.544
So believe it or not, the `else` in 
 Python's `while-else` is actually this `else`

00:02:06.544 --> 00:02:09.920
in the if-go-to implementation 
 of a while-loop.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.355
If there was a break in the while-loop,

00:02:11.355 --> 00:02:15.360
that would correspond to a go-to to&nbsp;
the corresponding end label.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.960
At the time it was expected 
 that&nbsp;this would be intuitive to users.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.228
That idea obviously didn't 
 age that well.

00:02:22.228 --> 00:02:25.680
But like&nbsp;it or not in modern Python 
 that is what it means.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.184
And likewise, the `else` in `for-else`&nbsp;
was meant for consistency with while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.184 --> 00:02:34.640
Alternatively you could think about it in terms&nbsp;
of how `for`s are implemented in terms of `while`s.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.884
So there's the first annoyance.

00:02:36.884 --> 00:02:40.919
The modern Python&nbsp;programmer doesn't really 
 think about for and while loops in terms of go-to's.

00:02:40.919 --> 00:02:44.400
Python doesn't&nbsp;even have 
 a go-to that's accessible to you!

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.259
There was at some point some discussion

00:02:46.259 --> 00:02:50.021
that maybe it should just be called 
 no-break instead of else.

00:02:50.021 --> 00:02:52.332
This is clearer&nbsp;and more in 
 line with what it does.

00:02:52.332 --> 00:02:54.408
So there'd be way less confusion 
 around it.

00:02:54.408 --> 00:02:57.384
But&nbsp;at this point, we can't 
 really change the else.

00:02:57.384 --> 00:03:00.192
For reasons we're about to talk about you&nbsp;
shouldn't really be using this anyway.

00:03:00.192 --> 00:03:02.683
But if you do choose to use 
 the else clause,

00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:06.211
at the very least,&nbsp; include a comment 
 to help jog the reader's memory.

00:03:06.211 --> 00:03:10.161
So theoretically, when might 
 you&nbsp;actually be tempted to use this?

00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:12.576
The most common situation 
 that&nbsp;I see is to avoid

00:03:12.576 --> 00:03:17.079
a single extra "unnecessary" 
 flag variable.

00:03:17.079 --> 00:03:19.578
Here we have a&nbsp;
 simple index implementation.

00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:21.869
index takes in a sequence like a list

00:03:21.869 --> 00:03:26.953
and a target value&nbsp;and tells you 
 where in the list that value is at what index.

00:03:26.953 --> 00:03:30.868
We create a found flag to tell&nbsp;us 
 whether or not we've found the target element.

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:32.334
then we loop over the sequence.

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:35.440
If we find&nbsp;the value, then 
 we set found to true and break.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.956
Then at the end we either raise or return the&nbsp;
index that we found the element at.

00:03:38.956 --> 00:03:43.920
The value of found at the end of the loop is always equal&nbsp;
to whether or not we broke out of the loop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.262
Since we can detect whether 
 we broke out of loop&nbsp;using a for-else,

00:03:47.262 --> 00:03:49.667
we technically don't need the 
 found variable.

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:52.347
Not found. Is just when 
 we broke out of the loop normally.

00:03:52.347 --> 00:03:54.160
So we can just use else.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.400
Cleaner and simpler, right?

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.205
Well, simpler than that other 
 implementation.

00:04:00.205 --> 00:04:06.182
Aut why don't we just take this return. 
 And put it right where the break is?

00:04:06.182 --> 00:04:09.379
Then we don't need an else at all.

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:14.240
And this is the second major reason why 
 for-else and while-else have aged so poorly.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.332
The vast majority of the time, you can 
 get rid of a for-else or while-else just by refactoring.

00:04:19.332 --> 00:04:21.066
Take your loop that you're going 
 to break out of.

00:04:21.066 --> 00:04:23.555
Pull it out into a function like 
 what we have here.

00:04:23.555 --> 00:04:25.888
And replace breaks with returns.

00:04:25.888 --> 00:04:27.407
Of course, it's not always possible.

00:04:27.407 --> 00:04:29.448
I know you can probably come 
 up with something.

00:04:29.448 --> 00:04:33.045
But in the vast majority of cases, 
 it is possible.

00:04:33.045 --> 00:04:35.735
And it's the same story for 
 the while-else.

00:04:35.735 --> 00:04:39.952
You know, writing this example, I actually 
 realized how little I actually use while-loops.

00:04:39.952 --> 00:04:45.361
I pretty much always think in terms of containers and iterables 
 and things that you use with for-loops.

00:04:45.362 --> 00:04:47.151
Okay, so here's the situation.

00:04:47.151 --> 00:04:52.654
Besides while-true which I do use that a while-loop 
 is actually better suited than a for-loop.

00:04:52.654 --> 00:04:56.940
I start out at some time and want 
 to do work for five seconds.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.000
While the current time is less than the target time,  
I do some work and then I sleep for a little bit.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.901
Let's just suppose that at some time 
 there's a possibility of some random error occurring.

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:09.269
Not an exception, just some way 
 of detecting that an error has happened.

00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:11.006
We break out of the loop 
 if an error occurs.

00:05:11.006 --> 00:05:14.729
And once again we use a flag to keep 
 track of whether or not we broke out of the loop.

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:17.593
Then we can either handle the error 
 or print out a "DONE" message.

00:05:17.593 --> 00:05:20.333
If we run it, we see 
 working, working, working, working.

00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:24.197
And then after three seconds, 
 we hit an error and handle the error.

00:05:24.197 --> 00:05:27.537
Once again, theoretically this error 
 flag is unnecessary.

00:05:27.537 --> 00:05:30.195
And we can get rid of it using 
 a while-else.

00:05:30.195 --> 00:05:31.178
We do that like this.

00:05:31.178 --> 00:05:33.227
If there was no break, that 
 means no error.

00:05:33.227 --> 00:05:36.000
So, we just print our done message 
 and return.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.479
If there was a break, then we skip 
 the return and handle the error.

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:41.330
Personally, I think this is way less readable.

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.587
I would prefer to just have the error flag.

00:05:43.587 --> 00:05:46.378
All this is done in the name 
 of performance.

00:05:46.378 --> 00:05:49.870
But this is going to be a huge readability 
 cost for what extra performance.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.002
Really! Saving one extra boolean?

00:05:53.002 --> 00:05:57.005
The performance benefit is going to be 
 minuscule at best if you can even measure it.

00:05:57.005 --> 00:05:59.015
The only time something like this 
 could make a difference

00:05:59.015 --> 00:06:01.215
is if you were doing it inside 
 of a hot loop.

00:06:01.215 --> 00:06:04.074
And even then the savings are 
 still going to be less than a percent.

00:06:04.074 --> 00:06:08.418
So please join me in actively avoiding 
 for-else and while-else.

00:06:08.418 --> 00:06:10.960
Personally, I rarely see for-else 
 actually used.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.168
And I have not once ever seen 
 while-else used in production code.

00:06:15.168 --> 00:06:18.058
Although even the creator of Python
 thinks that it was a bad idea

00:06:18.058 --> 00:06:20.525
that it was ever put into 
 the language in the first place.

00:06:20.525 --> 00:06:22.525
It's probably never going 
 to be removed.

00:06:22.525 --> 00:06:27.128
That would be a major backwards 
incompatibility requiring a Python 4-0 release.

00:06:27.128 --> 00:06:29.290
From the Python 2 to Python 3 transition,

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.616
the Python community already 
 learned how much of a hassle this is.

00:06:32.616 --> 00:06:34.870
So in the future, it's unlikely that 
 it'll get removed.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.532
But there are still some things 
 that I would recommend for you.

00:06:37.532 --> 00:06:41.442
Of course stop using it. And tell 
 your friends and coworkers to stop using it.

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:44.564
And politely suggest your DevOps 
 or Python infrastructure team

00:06:44.564 --> 00:06:49.450
to either turn on or create a linter 
 rule to prevent you from doing this.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.465
If you'd like to hear more about creating 
 your own linter rules, check out my video on the topic.

00:06:53.465 --> 00:06:56.154
And finally, I just want to say 
 that all this bad stuff about `else`s

00:06:56.154 --> 00:06:59.239
is only for for-else and while-else.

00:06:59.239 --> 00:07:02.103
I have nothing against if-else 
 or try-except-else.

00:07:02.103 --> 00:07:06.021
In fact, I think try-except-else is 
 actually very underutilized.

00:07:06.021 --> 00:07:08.289
Anyway, and as always thank 
 you for watching!

